package types

import text from "std/text"

// The queries under the binding in force, Go's: every question Go's
// Table answers about a type, it answers about what the type stands for
// under the binding in force (table.go:632), its Bound, which may add
// the types the binding makes (see bind.tck). Here each such query has
// a twin, bound_ and its name, which binds the type as Go's entry does
// and then answers about what it stands for, adding what Go's adds, in
// Go's order. The queries without the prefix answer about the type
// they are given, as Go's do with nothing bound: the type checker,
// which never binds, asks those, and with nothing bound a twin answers
// as they do, adding nothing. The twins are this package's own: code
// generation reaches them through a Bound (see bound.tck), which holds
// the Table and gives out no other query, so no query it makes can skip
// the binding.
//
// Each twin keeps what its Go query asks of the type itself before
// binding it, as InAny, IsStruct, HasZero and Fields do. A query Go's
// Table answers about a type's parts, as Owned, Size and Name do, binds
// each part again; the parts of what Bound gave are
// bound already, and bind declines any binding under which binding
// them again would change them (see unbindable), so a twin binds once,
// first, and its answer, and what it adds, are Go's. HasTypeParam and
// NameWith, which bind twice at every step, are bound_has_type_param
// and name_with, which do so too.

// bound_is_func is is_func under the binding in force, Go's IsFunc.
func (mut t Table) bound_is_func(typ Type) (bool, error) {
    b := mut t.bound(typ)?
    return t.is_func(b), noerror
}

// bound_is_union is is_union under the binding in force, Go's IsUnion.
func (mut t Table) bound_is_union(typ Type) (bool, error) {
    b := mut t.bound(typ)?
    return t.is_union(b), noerror
}

